.TH RSYNCFS 1 "%DATE%" "v%VERSION%"
.SH NAME
rsyncfs \- A FUSE filesystem that lets you mount rsync servers.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B
rsyncfs
[OPTION]... RSYNC_SERVER MOUNTPOINT
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rsyncfs
is a FUSE filesystem that lets you mount rsync servers. This project was forked
from https://github.com/zaddach/fuse-rsync and has been overhauled to address a
number of issues and make it less painful to use. Most notably, I/O performance
has been improved by making downloads asynchronous and adding extensive
caching.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \--help
Show the documentation and exit.
.TP
.B \-c COUNT
Maximum number of file metadata entries cached in memory. When this is 0, the
number of cached entries is unbounded.
.TP
.B \-e COMMAND
Path or name of the rsync executable.
.TP
.B \-p FILE
Path of the file containing the rsync server password.
.TP
.B \-q
Decrease logging verbosity. Can be specified repeatedly to further decrease
verbosity.
.TP
.B \-t TTL_SEC
Number of seconds file metadata is cached in memory. When this is 0, the items
never expire.
.TP
.B \-v
Increase logging verbosity. Can be specified repeatedly to further increase
verbosity.
.PP
In addition to these options, the FUSE library also accepts a number of its own
values. Refer to the output of "--help" for the full option list.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mount an unauthenticated rsync server:

.RS
.nf
$ rsyncfs rsync://gutenberg.pglaf.org/ /mnt/project-gutenberg
.fi
.RE

Mount a server that requires a password:

.RS
.nf
$ RSYNC_PASSWORD=hunter2 rsyncfs ericpruitt@storage45.local/backups/ ~/mounts/backups
.br
$ rsyncfs -p ~/secrets/nas-password ericpruitt@storage45.local/backups/ ~/mounts/backups
.fi
.RE

The password can also be specified in the URL, but this is not recommended since
specifying passwords on the command line may make them visible to other users
and/or processes on the same system:

.RS
.nf
$ rsyncfs ericpruitt:hunter2@nas.local/backups/ ~/mounts/backups
.fi
.RE
